"Alpha and Beta Decay | VirtualDemos",
"Alpha and Beta Decay",
"<strong>Views in the demo:</strong> Many nuclei (the Measuring a Half Life and Parent and Daughter worksheets); Alpha decay and Beta decay (this worksheet), which show one nucleus at a time.",
"Alpha decay has twenty even–even alpha emitters, from samarium {#} to californium {#}. For the chosen one it shows the potential energy of the alpha particle against its distance from the center of the daughter nucleus, the Geiger–Nuttall plot of all twenty, and a readout table: Q, the alpha energy, the radius R where the alpha and the daughter touch, the barrier top, the turning point b, the Gamow factor, the model and measured half lives and their ratio.",
"The model is Gamow’s ({#}): the alpha rattles inside and hits the wall \\(f = v/2R\\) times a second, and at each hit it tunnels through the Coulomb barrier with the WKB chance \\(e^{-2G}\\). R is \\(r_0(A_d^{1/3} + 4^{1/3})\\), and the nuclear radius constant \\(r_0\\) can be changed from {#} to {#} fm. Masses (for Q), half lives and the alpha branch are from the NUBASE2020 evaluation; the measured half life is the partial alpha half life, the half life divided by the share of decays that are alpha decays.",
"Watch it decay waits a random time drawn from the measured half life, at a scale where one mean life takes {#} s.",
"Beta decay has five beta emitters: the free neutron, tritium, carbon {#}, phosphorus {#} and fluorine {#} (a positron emitter). Each decay draws the electron’s energy from the allowed spectrum with the nonrelativistic Fermi function, and the histogram of your decays builds against the spectrum curve. One decay makes one; Run makes them continuously. The No neutrino setting shows what two body decay would give.",
"Record reading stores one emitter: in the Alpha decay view its Q, r₀ and the measured and model half lives in seconds; in the Beta decay view its Q, the number of decays so far, their mean energy and the mean of the spectrum. Export to worksheet sends alpha readings to Table {#} and beta readings to Table {#}; Clear starts a new series, which goes into a new copy of the table.",
"Test Gamow’s tunneling model of alpha decay against twenty measured half lives that span {#} powers of ten, find the nuclear radius constant that fits them, and use the energies of beta electrons to show that a third particle, the neutrino, carries energy away.",
"<strong>Alpha decay.</strong> A heavy nucleus can lower its energy by sending out an alpha particle, a helium {#} nucleus. The energy released, Q, comes from the masses. Momentum is shared with the daughter, so the alpha’s kinetic energy is \\(T_\\alpha = Q\\,m_d/(m_d + m_\\alpha)\\), a little less than Q, and the same in every decay.",
"Outside the nucleus the alpha feels the Coulomb repulsion of the daughter, of charge \\(Z_d e\\):",
"\\[V(r) = \\frac{2Z_d\\,e^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0\\,r}, \\qquad \\frac{e^2}{4\\pi\\varepsilon_0} = 1.440\\ \\mathrm{MeV\\,fm}.\\]",
"At the nuclear surface R this barrier is about {#} MeV for uranium, far above Q (about {#} to {#} MeV). Classically the alpha can never get out. Quantum mechanically it tunnels through, from R to the turning point \\(b = 2Z_d e^2/(4\\pi\\varepsilon_0 Q)\\) where \\(V = Q\\) (§{#}). The chance per hit on the barrier is \\(e^{-2G}\\), with the Gamow factor",
"\\[G = \\frac{\\sqrt{2\\mu Q}}{\\hbar}\\,b\\left[\\arccos\\sqrt{R/b} - \\sqrt{(R/b)(1 - R/b)}\\right],\\]",
"where \\(\\mu\\) is the reduced mass of the alpha and the daughter. With f hits a second, the decay constant is \\(\\lambda = f e^{-2G}\\) and \\(T_{1/2} = \\ln 2/\\lambda\\). Because G is in an exponent, a small change in Q changes the half life enormously.",
"When \\(R \\ll b\\) the bracket is close to \\(\\pi/2\\), and \\(2G\\) is proportional to \\(Z_d/\\sqrt{Q}\\). So \\(\\log_{10} T_{1/2}\\) is nearly a straight line against \\(Z_d/\\sqrt{Q}\\): the Geiger–Nuttall rule, found by experiment in {#}, before anyone knew why.",
"<strong>Beta decay.</strong> In \\(\\beta^-\\) decay a neutron in the nucleus becomes a proton, and an electron comes out; in \\(\\beta^+\\) decay a proton becomes a neutron and a positron comes out. If only the electron and the daughter came out, momentum and energy would fix the electron’s energy at nearly Q every time, as for the alpha. Instead the electrons come out with every energy from {#} to Q. Pauli proposed ({#}) that a third particle, the neutrino (an antineutrino in \\(\\beta^-\\) decay), takes the rest. For an allowed decay the spectrum is",
"\\[N(T) \\propto F(Z_d, T)\\,p\\,E\\,(Q - T)^2,\\]",
"with p and E the electron’s momentum and total energy and F the Fermi function, which accounts for the daughter’s charge pulling the electron back (or pushing a positron away). For a slow electron and no Coulomb effect \\(pE \\propto \\sqrt{T}\\), and the mean kinetic energy is exactly Q/{#}.",
"Uranium {#} alpha decays to thorium {#} with Q = {#} MeV. Find the alpha’s kinetic energy, taking the masses as {#} u and {#} u. Where does the rest of Q go?",
"For uranium {#} (\\(Z_d = 90\\), \\(A_d = 234\\)) with \\(r_0 = 1.20\\) fm, find R, the barrier height V(R) and the turning point b, in fm and MeV.",
"Show that for \\(R \\ll b\\), \\(\\log_{10} T_{1/2}\\) grows linearly with \\(Z_d/\\sqrt{Q}\\), and that the slope is \\(2\\pi(1.440\\ \\mathrm{MeV\\,fm})\\sqrt{2\\mu c^2}/(\\hbar c\\,\\ln 10)\\). Evaluate it for \\(\\mu c^2 = 3727\\) MeV and \\(\\hbar c = 197.3\\) MeV fm, with Q in MeV.",
"A neutron at rest decays into a proton and an electron only (no third particle). Using momentum conservation, explain why the electron would always have the same energy.",
"For the spectrum shape \\(N(T) \\propto \\sqrt{T}\\,(Q - T)^2\\) on \\(0 < T < Q\\), show that the mean kinetic energy is Q/{#}. (Expand \\((Q - T)^2\\) and integrate term by term.)",
"No hazards in the virtual lab. Real alpha emitters are dangerous if swallowed or breathed in; real beta sources are handled with shielding and tongs.",
"<strong>Twenty alpha emitters.</strong> With \\(r_0\\) at {#} fm, record a reading for each of the twenty alpha emitters.",
"<strong>Fitting the radius constant.</strong> Export and clear the readings. For each of ¹⁴⁸Gd, ²¹⁰Po, ²¹²Po, ²¹⁸Po, ²²²Rn, ²²⁶Ra, ²³²Th, ²³⁸U, ²³⁸Pu and ²⁵²Cf, find the \\(r_0\\), to {#} fm, at which the model half life comes closest to the measured one, and record a reading there. Export them to a new copy of Table {#}.",
